## Abstract

This thesis constructs a unified mathematical theory of great-power competition as a system of coupled stochastic differential equations with partial observability, in which artificial intelligence acts as a threshold-distributed modifier of the state-evolution rules themselves rather than a mere multiplier of existing capacity. We argue that contemporary geopolitics is governed not by territory or ideology but by the differential ability to scale intelligence production through physical infrastructure — compute, energy, semiconductors — subject to irreducible information asymmetries, supply-chain network fragility, transnational talent flows, multipolar perturbations, adversarial data operations, and heavy-tailed stochastic shocks. The AI capability threshold is modeled not as a fixed scalar but as a distribution over possible transition points, reflecting the empirical finding that capability emergence depends on model architecture, data quality, and training dynamics, not on compute alone. Perception layers are governed by nonlinear particle filters with adversarial observation channels, distinguishing random noise from deliberate deception. Manufacturing depth is replaced by a directed supply-chain graph with formally computed vulnerability metrics. Human capital is a transnational flow variable responsive to visa policy. Data is a state variable with adversarial poisoning dynamics. Stochastic shocks are Lévy-distributed, not Gaussian. The conflict boundary is formally defined as a codimension-1 hypersurface in the joint state space. A six-work-package deployment architecture specifies every human operation required to validate, calibrate, and operationalize the theory, including a 2018–2022 hindcast validation criterion. The result is a falsifiable, empirically grounded, and policy-actionable theory of power for the AI era.

**Keywords:** geopolitics, dynamical systems, stochastic differential equations, partial observability, AI threshold, supply chain vulnerability, talent flows, data poisoning, Lévy noise, mean first passage time, bifurcation, US–China competition.

