## Chapter 1: Introduction and Motivation

### 1.1 The Inadequacy of Existing Frameworks

The strategic environment of 2025–2026 has rendered classical geopolitical theory descriptively inadequate. The frameworks that dominated the twentieth century — balance-of-power theory, hegemonic stability theory, offense-defense theory, democratic peace theory — share a common deficiency: they treat power as an aggregated bundle of military capacity, economic output, and political will, assessed through observable signals and stable over time horizons of decades. None of them possesses the vocabulary to model what is now the central dynamic of great-power competition: the rate at which a nation can scale its production of artificial intelligence, constrained by physical infrastructure, disrupted by targeted supply-chain attacks, dependent on transnational talent pools, and obscured by structural opacity that prevents any actor from knowing the true capability of any rival.

At the core of AI geopolitics lies great-power rivalry where technological leadership is inseparable from strategic leadership, and the economic stakes are enormous — estimates suggest AI could add trillions to global GDP — but the distribution of gains will be uneven, privileging those with advanced digital ecosystems. [ORF Online](https://www.orfonline.org/research/the-geopolitics-of-ai-power-rivalry-and-the-remaking-of-global-order) This is not a merely economic observation; it is a claim about the mechanism by which power is produced and concentrated. The present thesis formalizes that mechanism.

### 1.2 The Empirical Landscape

Three interconnected empirical facts define the problem:

**First: Infrastructure chokepoints dominate.** In 2025, NVIDIA controlled 92% of the GPU market, cementing U.S. dominance in the processors that drive modern AI training and inference, while Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company commands roughly two-thirds of global foundry revenue, underscoring the world's reliance on a single geographic hub. [NYU](https://nyudri.org/publications/the-global-competition-for-dominance-in-artificial-intelligence/) Power, in the AI era, flows through identifiable physical bottlenecks.

**Second: The race is structurally opaque.** Neither the United States nor China can generate sufficient shared situational awareness about how AI is being developed, integrated, or used by the other. The most consequential components are often proprietary, classified, or otherwise inaccessible even to government analysts — hidden model architectures and parameters, undisclosed training data, uncertain compute access, privately conducted commercial R&D, and classified military applications. [Jhu](https://acf.sais.jhu.edu/in-ai-the-united-states-and-china-are-racing-in-the-dark/) Furthermore, China's domestic AI compute capacity is estimated at 1,882 quintillion calculations per second — vastly more than Western rankings suggest [South China Morning Post](https://www.scmp.com/news/china/science/article/3351037/chinas-dark-compute-power-could-be-6000-times-higher-current-estimates) , indicating not merely noise but deliberate strategic opacity in capability signaling.

**Third: Talent has become a contested strategic input.** At least 85 rising and established scientists working in the U.S. have joined Chinese research institutions full-time since early 2025, a reverse brain drain that experts say is poised to expand as the White House pushes to slash research budgets and steps up scrutiny of foreign talent, while Beijing increases investment in homegrown innovation. [CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2025/09/29/china/china-reverse-brain-drain-science-tech-competition-us-intl-hnk) Meanwhile, foreign-born workers now make up 22% of the overall U.S. STEM workforce and roughly 43% of doctorate-level professionals in science and engineering occupations, with international students earning 56% of U.S. PhDs in computer science and engineering. [ACS C&EN](https://cen.acs.org/policy/us-visa-restriction-stem-research-innovation-patent/104/web/2026/05) Talent is not a fixed national endowment; it is a flow variable governed by policy levers that are actively being pulled on both sides.

### 1.3 The Thesis and Its Claims

This thesis makes six structural claims, each instantiated in the formal framework:

1. **Intelligence production, not territory or ideology, is the primary determinant of contemporary geopolitical power**, and it can be expressed as a measurable, physically constrained rate.
2. **The international system is perception-mediated**: no actor observes the true state of any rival, and the coupling between states operates through filtered, noisy, adversarially manipulated estimates.
3. **AI is not a multiplier but a regime-changer**: beyond a critical compute density whose location is itself uncertain, artificial intelligence alters the eigenvalue structure of the system's Jacobian, not merely its rate.
4. **Manufacturing depth is a network property, not a scalar**: vulnerability is a function of supply-chain graph topology, and surgical chokepoint denial is more powerful than aggregate capacity reduction.
5. **The system is driven by heavy-tailed, not Gaussian, stochastic forcing**: geopolitical shocks are fat-tailed events that can cross basin boundaries even when deterministic drift favors stability.
6. **The theory is falsifiable**: it makes quantitative predictions about historical trajectories that can be tested against observed data.

