## Chapter 2: Theoretical Framework

### 2.1 The State Space

Let the international system consist of three nodes: the United States (US), the People's Republic of China (CN), and a Rest-of-World aggregate (RoW). Each node $i$ possesses a true state vector:

$$\mathbf{X}_i(t) = \big[ C_i,\ E_i,\ \mathcal{G}_{M,i},\ H_i,\ D_i,\ S_i \big]^\top$$

where $C_i(t)$ is compute capacity (aggregate FLOPs/year, expressed in GPU-H100-equivalents); $E_i(t)$ is digital-sector energy supply (TWh/year); $\mathcal{G}_{M,i}(t)$ is the semiconductor supply chain graph; $H_i(t)$ is the stock of active AI-focused researchers; $D_i(t)$ is the quality-weighted usable data corpus; and $S_i(t)$ is scientific output in AI-relevant fields.

The effective intelligence production capacity of node $i$ is:

$$I_i(t) = f\!\big(C_i,\ E_i,\ M_{\text{eff},i},\ H_i,\ D_i\big) \cdot A_i(t)$$

where $M_{\text{eff},i}$ is effective manufacturing capacity derived from $\mathcal{G}_{M,i}$ (see §2.3), and $A_i(t)$ is the AI regime-change function specified in §2.2.

### 2.2 The AI Acceleration Function: A Stochastic Threshold Model

Previous formalizations modeled the AI acceleration as a continuous function of compute, an assumption invalidated by the 2024–2026 scaling law literature. Emergent abilities are contingent on multiple interacting factors — model scale and architecture, data quality and diversity, training objectives — and emergence aligns more closely with pre-training loss landmarks than with sheer parameter count; smaller models can match larger ones if training loss is sufficiently reduced. [Emergent Mind](https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/emergent-abilities-of-large-language-models) More critically, discontinuities in capability are real — each model firmly either knows or does not know a given concept — but breakthroughs do not always reflect a fixed threshold at which a concept is learnable; instead, models may learn the concept at various scales, with changing probability. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.17356v5)

The appropriate formalization is therefore:

$$A_i(t) = \begin{cases} 1 + \alpha \cdot \log(1 + C_i/E_i), & C_i < C^* \\ A_{\text{jump}} \cdot \big(1 + \alpha \cdot \log(1 + C_i/E_i)\big)^{\beta}, & C_i \ge C^* \end{cases}$$

where $C^* \sim P(C^*)$ is a **random variable** drawn from an empirically inferred distribution over threshold compute levels. This distribution is estimated from the Epoch AI database of frontier model training runs, treating compute levels at which qualitatively new capabilities first appeared as samples from $P(C^*)$. The stochastic treatment of $C^*$ produces a probability distribution over bifurcation points — the probability that the system has **already crossed the threshold undetected** becomes a central, computable quantity of the model. This transforms the "racing in the dark" problem from a narrative observation into a formal probability statement.

When $C_i \geq C^*$, the function $\mathbf{\Phi}(A_i)$ — a sigmoid-like gate — activates new dynamical terms in the drift function $\mathbf{F}_i$ itself:

$$\mathbf{F}_i(\mathbf{X}_i, \mathbf{U}_i, A_i) = \mathbf{F}_i^{\text{base}}(\mathbf{X}_i, \mathbf{U}_i) + \mathbf{\Phi}(A_i) \odot \mathbf{F}_i^{\text{AI}}(\mathbf{X}_i)$$

Above threshold, AI-driven scientific discovery injects a recursive term into $\dot{C}_i$, autonomous grid management improves energy efficiency, and AI-enabled cyber operations can accelerate the effective depreciation of a rival's compute stock. This makes the Jacobian $\partial \mathbf{F}_i / \partial \mathbf{X}_i$ a function of $A_i$: the local stability of any equilibrium can abruptly shift when the threshold is crossed — an endogenous structural break in the competitive regime.

### 2.3 Supply Chain Graph and Effective Manufacturing Capacity

Manufacturing depth is replaced by a directed, weighted graph $\mathcal{G}_{M,i}(t)$. Nodes represent firms and processes in the semiconductor supply chain: logic design houses (Qualcomm, HiSilicon), foundries (TSMC, Samsung, SMIC, Intel Foundry), lithography equipment manufacturers (ASML, Canon, Nikon), materials suppliers, and packaging firms. Edges carry weights reflecting annual transaction volumes in USD and technology dependency classifications (irreplaceable vs. substitutable).

The node criticality index $V_i^{(k)}$ for node $k$ in country $i$'s supply chain is defined as:

$$V_i^{(k)} = \frac{\text{throughput lost upon removal of }k}{\text{total system throughput}}$$

computed via betweenness centrality and simulated node removal analysis. The aggregate supply chain vulnerability $V_i = \max_k V_i^{(k)}$ captures the worst-case single point of failure. Effective manufacturing capacity is then:

$$M_{\text{eff},i} = M_{\text{gross},i} \cdot (1 - \kappa \cdot V_i)$$

where $\kappa$ is calibrated from historical supply disruptions. The intricate web of global semiconductor supply chains is increasingly being torn apart by escalating geopolitical tensions, driven primarily by the fierce technological rivalry between the United States and China, with profound and immediate consequences for the development and availability of AI technologies worldwide. [Chroniclejournal](https://markets.chroniclejournal.com/chroniclejournal/article/tokenring-2025-11-6-the-silicon-schism-geopolitics-reshapes-global-ai-future) The $V_i$ metric formalizes this fragility. Interdependence in the U.S.–China semiconductor supply chain linkages further complicates their rivalry, as it is heavily weaponized by both states through the means of economic statecraft, which includes sanctions, tariffs, policies, and shifts in economic alliances. [Ceu](https://www.etd.ceu.edu/2025/maksakova_anastasia.pdf)

### 2.4 Transnational Talent Dynamics

Human capital is modeled as a flow variable:

$$\frac{dH_i}{dt} = r_i(\mathbf{U}_i) + \sum_{j \neq i} m_{j \to i}(\mathbf{U}_i, \mathbf{U}_j) - \sum_{j \neq i} m_{i \to j}(\mathbf{U}_i, \mathbf{U}_j) - \delta H_i$$

where $r_i$ is the domestic production rate of AI researchers, $m_{j \to i}$ is the migration flow from $j$ to $i$ (a function of relative visa openness, research funding, and political climate), and $\delta$ is attrition. This is not an abstraction. China produces nearly half of the world's AI talent compared to the U.S., which accounts for 18%, and China has consistently ranked as the U.S.'s biggest and most important source of high-level international STEM workers. [Rest of World](https://restofworld.org/2024/china-us-immigration-policy-ai-talent/) At the same time, Chinese universities see changes in the U.S. as a gift from Trump that will help them recruit more and higher-caliber talents, with at least 85 scientists having already moved full-time to Chinese institutions in 2025 alone. [CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2025/09/29/china/china-reverse-brain-drain-science-tech-competition-us-intl-hnk)

The policy control input $\mathbf{U}_i$ directly enters the talent dynamics: China's State Council announced in August 2025 a K visa for eligible young science and technology professionals, effective October 1, 2025, making talent attracting less about hospitality and more about technological statecraft. [Synergiafoundation](https://synergiafoundation.org/2026/05/01/k-is-for-competition/) Meanwhile on the U.S. side, F-1 application refusal rates reached a decade high of 35% and J-1 visa issuances declined 13% in May 2025 alone, with new proposals to eliminate Optional Practical Training and impose fixed durations of stay actively driving talent away. [ACS C&EN](https://cen.acs.org/policy/us-visa-restriction-stem-research-innovation-patent/104/web/2026/05) These empirical dynamics are directly instantiated in the $m_{j \to i}$ functions of our model.

### 2.5 Data Dynamics with Adversarial Poisoning

The data state variable $D_i(t)$ has its own evolution equation:

$$\frac{dD_i}{dt} = \gamma_i \cdot H_i(t) - \lambda \cdot D_i(t) - \psi_i \cdot P_{ji}(t) + \eta_i(t)$$

where $\gamma_i$ is the per-researcher data generation rate; $\lambda$ is the natural decay rate from synthetic data recirculation and model collapse; $P_{ji}(t)$ is the adversarial data poisoning rate injected by rival $j$; $\psi_i$ is susceptibility to poisoning; and $\eta_i(t)$ is a stochastic data shock term.

The adversarial poisoning term is not speculative. Data poisoning involves introducing corrupted or adversarial data into the training sets of machine learning models, causing them to operate unpredictably, and represents a reciprocal threat that adversaries are actively developing — while the United States may pursue covert means to degrade adversary AI reliability, it must also anticipate parallel efforts targeting its own systems. [Lieber Institute West Point](https://lieber.westpoint.edu/data-poisoning-covert-weapon-securing-us-military-superiority-ai-driven-warfare/) Furthermore, adversarial actors are already poisoning AI training data, and other research from 2025 found that several organizations had been victims of AI data poisoning. [Defense](https://media.defense.gov/2026/Mar/04/2003882809/-1/-1/0/AI_ML_SUPPLY_CHAIN_RISKS_AND_MITIGATIONS.PDF) A country that can degrade a rival's training data distribution achieves slow, undetectable capability suppression — a new geopolitical weapon this framework uniquely captures.

### 2.6 The Full SDE System with Partial Observability and Lévy Noise

**True state dynamics.** The system is governed by:

$$d\mathbf{X}_i = \mathbf{F}_i(\mathbf{X}_i, \mathbf{U}_i, A_i)\, dt + \sum_{j \neq i} \mathbf{G}_{ij}\big(\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{j}^{(i)}, \hat{\mathbf{X}}_{i}^{(j)}\big)\, dt + \Sigma_i\, d\mathbf{L}_i(t)$$

The noise term $d\mathbf{L}_i(t)$ is a Lévy process — heavy-tailed, capable of generating the large discontinuous jumps that characterize geopolitical shocks (wars, sanctions, technological breakthroughs, leadership changes). Gaussian white noise is inappropriate here: for systems driven by non-Gaussian, α-stable noise, the classical early warning signs of rising variance and autocorrelation are not supported by mathematical theory, and their use poses the danger of spurious, false-positive results. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.16350) The Lévy specification is empirically justified by the fat-tailed distribution of geopolitical shock magnitudes documented across the 1945–2025 record.

**Perception dynamics.** No actor observes any rival's true state. Each actor $i$ maintains a particle-filter estimate of actor $j$'s state, $\hat{\mathbf{X}}_j^{(i)}(t)$:

$$\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{j}^{(i)}(t) = \sum_{k=1}^{N_p} w_k^{(i,j)}(t) \cdot \mathbf{x}_k(t)$$

where particles $\mathbf{x}_k$ are propagated through the estimated dynamics and reweighted by the likelihood of the incoming observation stream. A linear Kalman filter is inappropriate here because the true dynamics are strongly nonlinear (piecewise AI threshold, network vulnerability, talent flows) and because intelligence observations are non-Gaussian.

**Adversarial observation model.** The observation stream itself is not merely noisy. It may be adversarially manipulated:

$$\mathbf{y}_j(t) = \mathbf{H}\mathbf{X}_j(t) + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\text{random}}(t) + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\text{adversarial}}(\mathbf{U}_j^{\text{deception}}, t)$$

This distinguishes two fundamentally different sources of perception error: random noise (handled by the particle filter) and deliberate deception (a policy choice with strategic cost-benefit tradeoffs). The coupling functions operate on perceived, not true states:

$$\mathbf{G}_{ij} = \mathbf{G}_{ij}\big(\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{j}^{(i)},\ \hat{\mathbf{X}}_{i}^{(j)}\big)$$

This embedding of perception into the dynamical coupling is the theoretical core of the model. Stable relations in the age of AI require new intellectual frameworks, not recycled Cold War logic, with updated mechanisms designed for a world in which opacity and rapid change are features, not bugs. [Jhu](https://acf.sais.jhu.edu/in-ai-the-united-states-and-china-are-racing-in-the-dark/) The present framework provides exactly this.

### 2.7 Multipolar Coupling: The Rest-of-World Node

A third aggregate node $\mathbf{X}_{\text{RoW}}(t)$ represents the European Union, India, Gulf states, and other actors:

$$d\mathbf{X}_{\text{RoW}} = \mathbf{F}_{\text{RoW}}\, dt + \mathbf{G}_{\text{RoW,US}} + \mathbf{G}_{\text{RoW,CN}} + \Sigma_{\text{RoW}}\, d\mathbf{L}_{\text{RoW}}$$

This third body can stabilize (by diversifying chip supply, as TSMC's Arizona expansion does) or destabilize (by aligning with one pole and shifting perceived advantage). Between 2017 and 2025, the U.S. shipped 40 foundation models, China 15, and Europe only 3 — and there isn't one European AI plan, there are 27, each with its own priorities, timelines, and budgets, meaning the EU's collective weight is fragmented. [Declic Media](https://www.declic.media/en/news/chine-dark-compute-strategie-long-terme/) This fragmentation is captured in the RoW node's internal heterogeneity parameter. Sensitivity analysis over RoW policy parameters reveals whether a third-node alignment shift can eliminate or merely displace the bifurcation boundaries of the US–CN dyad.

### 2.8 The Conflict Regime: Formal Definition

The mean first passage time computation requires a mathematically defined absorbing boundary. We define conflict onset as the moment the system trajectory crosses a codimension-1 hypersurface $\mathcal{S}$ in the stacked state space characterized by two simultaneous conditions:

**Condition 1 — Perceived Power Gap:**

$$\big|\hat{I}_{\text{US}}^{(\text{CN})} - \hat{I}_{\text{CN}}^{(\text{US})}\big| > \theta_{\text{conflict}}$$

where hat notation denotes each side's *estimate* of the other's capacity. Conflict risk is driven by perceived, not actual, advantage — consistent with the empirical literature on deterrence failure.

**Condition 2 — Coupling Derivative:**

$$\frac{d}{dt}\big\|\mathbf{G}_{\text{US,CN}} + \mathbf{G}_{\text{CN,US}}\big\| < -\theta_{\text{decoupling}}$$

indicating that cooperative linkages are deteriorating at a rate that precludes diplomatic recovery.

This definition embeds the "fog of war" logic directly: real-world geopolitical crises involve intertwined dynamics across military strategy, economic incentives, diplomacy, domestic politics, and human perception, demanding the ability to navigate uncertainty, shifting incentives, and partial observability — the classic conditions described as the fog of war. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.16642) Our $\mathcal{S}$ manifold formalizes the conditions under which this fog produces irreversible escalation.

