## Chapter 4: Mathematical Analysis

### 4.1 The Fokker-Planck Equation for the Coupled System

For the SDE system with Lévy noise, the evolution of the probability density $p(\mathbf{X}, t)$ is governed by a generalized Fokker-Planck equation including a Lévy integral term:

$$\frac{\partial p}{\partial t} = -\nabla \cdot (\mathbf{F}\, p) + \frac{1}{2}\nabla\nabla : (\Sigma\Sigma^\top p) + \int_{\mathbb{R}^n \setminus \{0\}} \big[p(\mathbf{X}-\mathbf{z}) - p(\mathbf{X}) + \mathbf{z}\cdot\nabla p\big]\nu(d\mathbf{z})$$

where $\nu$ is the Lévy measure characterizing the jump intensity and size distribution. The stationary solutions of this equation define the long-run probability distribution over competitive regimes — equilibrium, decoupling, or conflict.

### 4.2 Stability Analysis under Threshold Uncertainty

Because $C^*$ is a random variable, the bifurcation analysis must be probabilistic. Define $\mathcal{P}_{\text{crossed}}(t)$ as the probability that node $i$ has crossed the threshold by time $t$:

$$\mathcal{P}_{\text{crossed}}^{(i)}(t) = \int_{-\infty}^{C_i(t)} p_{C^*}(c)\, dc = F_{C^*}(C_i(t))$$

This probability is directly observable: given estimates of a nation's current compute stock $C_i(t)$ and the distribution $P(C^*)$ inferred from historical emergence data, one can compute the likelihood that its AI systems have already entered the recursive self-improvement regime. When $\mathcal{P}_{\text{crossed}}^{(i)}(t) - \mathcal{P}_{\text{crossed}}^{(j)}(t)$ exceeds a threshold, the system enters an asymmetric regime in which the leading actor's vector field has changed while the lagging actor still perceives bilateral symmetry — a structural source of miscalculation.

### 4.3 Mean First Passage Time to the Conflict Boundary

The mean first passage time $\tau_{\mathcal{S}}$ for the system starting at state $\mathbf{X}_0$ to reach the conflict hypersurface $\mathcal{S}$ satisfies the Pontryagin backward equation:

$$\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{X}_0)\cdot\nabla\tau + \frac{1}{2}\text{tr}\big(\Sigma\Sigma^\top \nabla^2\tau\big) + \mathcal{L}_\nu[\tau] = -1$$

with boundary condition $\tau(\mathbf{X}) = 0$ for $\mathbf{X} \in \mathcal{S}$, where $\mathcal{L}_\nu$ is the non-local Lévy generator. This equation is solved numerically via Monte Carlo simulation (Euler-Maruyama with $N = 10{,}000$ realizations). The output is a map of $\tau_{\mathcal{S}}(\mathbf{X}_0, \mathbf{U})$ — the expected time to conflict onset as a function of starting conditions and policy choices.

### 4.4 Early Warning Signals under Lévy Noise

Classical early-warning indicators (rising variance, rising lag-1 autocorrelation) are invalid for Lévy-driven systems. We adopt instead the generalized scaling factor $\gamma_X$, which satisfies a direct inverse relationship with the distance to bifurcation even under heavy-tailed noise: the scaling factor $\gamma_X$ provides a generalized approach that works for both Gaussian and non-Gaussian α-stable noise, exhibiting a direct inverse relationship with the bifurcation parameter, valid where classical indicators produce spurious false-positive results. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12012112/)

A composite early-warning index is constructed from $\gamma_X$ supplemented by: skewness and excess kurtosis of the state distribution (rising heavy tails signal increasing probability of extreme excursions); return-time shortening (decreasing mean time between threshold crossings); and the coupling derivative $d\|\mathbf{G}_{ij}\|/dt$. A multi-signal confirmation logic ("warning" requires two indicators; "alert" requires three) bounds the false alarm rate.

