## Chapter 6: Institutional Architecture and Required Resources

### 6.1 Team Composition

This research program requires a multidisciplinary team. In mathematical foundations: two to three applied mathematicians specializing in SDEs, Lévy processes, nonlinear dynamics, and bifurcation theory. In computational methods: two computational social scientists experienced in Bayesian state-space modeling, particle filtering, and MCMC. In supply chain analysis: one to two network scientists for graph construction and vulnerability analysis. In strategic context: one to two international relations scholars with expertise in US–China technology competition, to ensure theoretical validity and policy translation. In data infrastructure: one data engineer and one research coordinator.

### 6.2 Institutional Setting

The research should be housed in an institution combining access to unclassified and classified data streams (or a government partnership), interdisciplinary capacity bridging mathematics and strategic studies, convening power for scenario exercises involving academic researchers, policy practitioners, and industry experts, and independence from any single government.

### 6.3 Data Access Requirements

Formal agreements are required with Epoch AI for frontier model training run data; the Semiconductor Industry Association and SEMI for supply chain data; national statistical agencies for energy, STEM education, and trade flow data; bibliometric databases (Web of Science, Scopus); and corporate disclosure databases for TSMC, Samsung, Intel, SMIC, ASML, and NVIDIA.

