## Chapter 8: The Central Thesis, Restated

Modern geopolitical power is no longer an abstract bundle of military and economic capacity. It is a physically measurable, rate-limited process of intelligence scaling whose stability boundaries can be computed, monitored, and anticipated. Artificial intelligence does not simply amplify existing power — it rewires the feedback structure of the international system, compressing the time constants that separate advantage from dominance, and doing so at a threshold that no actor can locate with certainty. The result is a system that is not merely competitive but constitutively unstable: the very act of estimating a rival's capabilities generates misperception that feeds back into the dynamics. The opacity is not a solvable information problem — it is baked into the structure of the competition, as AI is not merely another variable in great-power competition, and the United States and China are, in a deep sense, racing in the dark, with neither able to generate sufficient situational awareness about the other's true capabilities. [Jhu](https://acf.sais.jhu.edu/in-ai-the-united-states-and-china-are-racing-in-the-dark/)

A theory that fails to model this opacity, this threshold uncertainty, and this heavy-tailed shock structure is not merely incomplete. It is actively misleading, because it gives policymakers the false comfort of a legible, predictable rivalry when the actual system is capable of generating catastrophic surprises at any time. What this thesis offers instead is not a prediction but a map of the conditions under which surprise is probable, the policy levers that shift those conditions, and the early-warning signatures that announce an approaching boundary.

The stakes of getting this right are not academic.

